{"id": "38745", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=38745", "title": "Weaverville (California)", "text": "Weaverville is in Trinity County, which is an inland part of California's North Coast region. It sits at 2188 feet of elevation.\nGet in.\nBy car.\n &amp; Highway 299 runs through the center of town on main street. Highway 3 runs through the town as well heading North towards Yreka.\nBy plane.\nThere is a small airport in the NE part of the town at Lonnie Pool Field [dead link] (FAA Code: O54) for general aviation use only. Nearest commercial airports served only by United Express are in Redding (54 mi east) and McKinleyville (100 mi west).\nEat.\nThere is only one fast food franchise that is here in Weaverville and that is Burger King, a Subway opened up in the same shopping plaza but that is it for corporate food places other than Round Table.\nPizza.\nThere\u2019s only a couple of options in town and they are virtually right next to each other (only separated by the foundation of the old Roundtable Pizza that burnt down years ago):\nThe Water Bar at 100 Nugget Ln. makes excellent pizza on naan bread with fresh toppings piled on, served with a boba milk tea, Italian soda or a smoothie all made with their superb tasting alkaline Ph balanced water (hence the name of the place)\nTangle Blue at 130 Nugget Ln. is one of three local \u2018watering holes\u2019 (not to be confused with the actual Water Bar highlighted above). It, by far, has the coolest name out of the three and the coolest bartenders who will hook you up with your favorite libation and one of their artisan pizzas\nCope.\nHospitals.\nTrinity Hospital in Weaverville, is owned by Mountain Communities Healthcare, has a 24-hour emergency room\nStay safe.\nHas a very low crime rate does not have a police department but does have a County Sherriff and the California Highway Patrol. Also of note there are few gas stations outside of Weaverville so it would be wise to get gas there, as cell phone service is spotty as you are in the mountains and would not want to be stuck for a long period of time. Also be wary in the wintertime as it does snow in this area and usually the roads are cleaned up rather quickly, best advice would to bring chains for your vehicles tires just in case"}
{"id": "38746", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=38746", "title": "Weaverville (North Carolina)", "text": "Weaverville is a town in the North Carolina Mountains.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe Asheville Regional Airport () offers jet and commuter service on American, Delta Air Lines, and United Airlines and has direct flights from Atlanta, Charlotte, Cincinnati, Detroit, Houston, Minneapolis/St. Paul, Newark, NJ, and Orlando. It is located fifteen miles south of downtown Asheville near the town of Fletcher. Ground transportation and major rental car companies are available at the airport terminal.\nFlights into Greenville-Spartanburg International Airport () located in Greenville, South Carolina, Charlotte Douglas International Airport (CLT) located in Charlotte, North Carolina, or McGhee Tyson Airport (TYS) located in Knoxville, Tennessee, are sometimes cheaper than flying directly to Asheville. Greenville is about a 1\u00bd-hour drive, Charlotte and Knoxville are both about 2-hour drives.\nGet around.\nLike most of Western North Carolina, you do need a car to get to and from Weaverville. Once on Main Street, it's an easy walk to much of the shopping, restaurants, and accommodations in the area. Limited bus service to and from Asheville is also available.\nDrink.\nAlcohol is served by the drink in several bars and restaurants, except on Sunday.\nBeer and wine are available for purchase at most markets, grocery stores and gas stations. The alcohol laws of North Carolina prohibit the sale of alcohol after 2AM Monday through Saturday, and from 2AM until noon on Sundays."}
